Native OpenZFS correctness and stress tests
=========================================

These tests exercise a real mounted filesystem through NetBSD system calls.
The older t_zpool test uses rump; it does not exercise the new native adapter.
The manual runner requires root, a securelevel of zero or less for the system
file-flag tests, and an existing disposable pool with at least 1 GiB free.
It creates and destroys only a uniquely named child of that pool.  It does
not partition disks or create or destroy pools.

Build h_zfs_stress with the NetBSD makefile in this directory, or natively:

    cc -O2 -g -Wall -Wextra -Werror -o h_zfs_stress h_zfs_stress.c
    sh ./run_native.sh scratchpool 1000

Keep the helper and script together.  Run from outside the test dataset.
The script uses /var/tmp for control files and a temporary mountpoint.
Each C process has a ten-minute alarm; this cannot recover a kernel deadlock.
Keep a console and a working debugger available for diagnostic testing.

CASES selects a space-separated subset.  Iterations apply to looped tests,
not to the fixed syscall checks or the 15 administrative-operation cases:

    CASES="coherence contention namespace" sh ./run_native.sh scratchpool 10000
    CASES="paging readonly lifecycle" sh ./run_native.sh scratchpool 1000

PRESSURE_MIB optionally holds that many MiB of touched, pageable anonymous
memory while the tests run, then verifies the anonymous pages before release.
Choose this explicitly for the machine's RAM and swap; it is disabled by
default.  For example, on a machine with 8 GiB RAM and adequate swap:

    PRESSURE_MIB=7168 CASES="paging contention namespace" \
        sh ./run_native.sh scratchpool 1000

Diagnostic build
----------------

The amd64 ZFSDEBUG kernel configuration includes GENERIC and enables DEBUG
and LOCKDEBUG; GENERIC already enables DIAGNOSTIC.  Build and boot that kernel
using build.sh's kernel=ZFSDEBUG target.

Build BOTH loadable modules in fresh object directories with matching kernel
options.  For example, from the source root on NetBSD:

    mkdir -p /var/obj/zfs-diagnostic/zfs /var/obj/zfs-diagnostic/solaris
    make -C sys/modules/solaris MAKEOBJDIR=/var/obj/zfs-diagnostic/solaris \
        COPTS="-O2 -g -DLOCKDEBUG -DDEBUG" dependall
    make -C sys/modules/zfs MAKEOBJDIR=/var/obj/zfs-diagnostic/zfs \
        COPTS="-O2 -g -DLOCKDEBUG -DDEBUG -DZFS_DEBUG" dependall

Install these together with the diagnostic kernel and the new zfs/zpool
commands.  Do not reuse release module objects: compiler flags are not make
dependencies, and ZFS_DEBUG changes internal structure layouts.

DEBUG enables ASSERT, ASSERT0 and ASSERT3* in the retained assertion headers.
ZFS_DEBUG additionally enables OpenZFS debug paths and debug refcounts.
The upstream debug default for zfs_flags enables the dbuf, dnode, metaslab
and other verification flags, except the logging/remap/reconstruction flags
excluded by spa_misc.c.  Global reference-holder tracking retains its
upstream disabled default; explicitly tracked refcounts still work.

Coverage and oracles
-------------------

* permissions: ownership, owner/group/other access, supplementary groups,
  denied chmod/chown, umask, sticky unlink and replacement, group inheritance,
  and clearing set-id bits after an unprivileged write.
* metadata: nanosecond timestamps, atime/mtime/ctime updates, hard/symbolic
  links, O_NOFOLLOW, pathconf/statvfs, maximum-length names, FIFOs, Unix
  sockets, directory moves and parent lookup, and invalid rename/rmdir.
* sparse: a 64 MiB hole checked byte-for-byte, allocation accounting, and
  zero filling after truncate/re-extension.
* holes: native FIOSEEKDATA/FIOSEEKHOLE ioctls, data and virtual EOF holes,
  and ENXIO at/beyond EOF.  Hole reporting is allowed to be conservative
  while transaction groups are dirty.
* flags: UF_NODUMP, SF_IMMUTABLE, SF_APPEND, denied mutation and flag clearing.
  ZFS represents immutable/append-only with system flags; the user variants
  UF_IMMUTABLE and UF_APPEND are not implemented by the current adapter.
* append: four independently opened append writers; every fixed-size record
  must appear intact exactly once and in each writer's sequence order.
* locking: fcntl range conflict/owner reporting, disjoint ranges, and flock
  conflicts across independent opens.
* coherence: pipe-synchronized processes alternate mmap stores and buffered
  writes to the same page.  Checks dirty mapping visibility before msync,
  msync/fsync, cached-page updates, partial-page truncation, zero extension,
  and MAP_PRIVATE isolation.
* lifetime: rename over a live file, hard links, unlink while open/mapped,
  access with only a mapping retaining the vnode, and SIGBUS beyond new EOF.
* contention: six processes mix buffered I/O and shared mmap/msync/fsync on
  disjoint pages of one file while the parent repeatedly truncates its tail.
  The disjoint ranges give exact data oracles without ambiguous write races.
* directories: enumeration during four workers' create/link/rename-over/
  unlink operations, with bounded iteration and a final empty-directory check.
* namespace: competing writers replace one pathname while readers open,
  hard-link, map, unlink and close it.  Opening the replacement pathname must
  never see a gap, and all bytes must belong to one complete version.
  A link source may become unlinked after source lookup and before the
  destination lock is acquired; ZFS rejects resurrection with ENOENT.
  These outcomes are counted separately, and each reader must successfully
  link and verify at least one version.
* paging: 256 MiB of deterministic mapped data, MS_SYNC|MS_INVALIDATE,
  buffered verification/rewrite, remapping, and a SHA256 comparison across
  dataset unmount/remount.
* readonly: mutation rejection in an automounted snapshot and a read-only
  dataset.  Mount updates are unsupported, so readonly is set while unmounted.
* lifecycle: separately retain an fd, a mapping without an fd, or a working
  directory.  Ordinary unmount/destruction must fail busy and leave access
  intact; rollback must restore data through retained fds/mappings.  Forced
  unmount/destruction must complete, followed by reference release.  This
  does not dereference revoked mappings after forced teardown.

The runner waits for a scrub and requires a healthy pool, then checks that
its dataset can be destroyed.  Failures stop the run and trigger cleanup.
Use the individual C cases in empty directories on FFS to check assumptions
about native syscall semantics.

These are bounded regression/stress tests, not an exhaustive VOP suite or
durability qualification.  NFS/filehandles, ACL/xattr interfaces, quotas,
zvols, device-node I/O, kqueue, crash/ZIL replay and long-duration low-memory
operation need separate coverage.

Recorded diagnostic run
-----------------------

Tested NetBSD/amd64 11.99.8 with eight CPUs, 8 GiB RAM, OpenZFS 2.4.4,
the ZFSDEBUG kernel, and both modules built with the options above:

* All default cases passed on a single-disk pool and a three-disk RAIDZ1
  pool, using 1,000 iterations for looped cases.  The hole-ioctl case was
  added and run separately on both pools.
* Append, coherence, contention and directory enumeration also passed
  10,000 iterations on the single-disk pool.
* RAIDZ1 paging, contention, namespace and all 15 lifecycle cases passed
  with 7,168 MiB of anonymous-memory pressure.  The pagedaemon freed over
  850,000 pages during this run; this exercised actual pageout.  The
  anonymous-memory verification also passed.
* Every pool scrub finished with zero errors.  Test datasets were removed,
  and both pools remained healthy.
* The filesystem-independent cases were also checked on native FFS.

Two adapter defects were found and corrected:

* Repeated dataset creation tripped LOCKDEBUG's "already initialized" panic
  in zfs_create_fs.  Its temporary zfsvfs was freed without destroying
  z_znodes_lock.  The temporary list and mutex are now both destroyed.
* Immutable-file open/truncate returned EACCES because zfs_netbsd_access
  translated every EPERM.  It now preserves EPERM for immutable writes,
  matching FFS.  The flag tests passed after the fix.

No further kernel assertions or LOCKDEBUG failures occurred in the completed
runs.  Setting readonly on an already mounted dataset still fails its mount
update and prints a misleading version-upgrade diagnostic; unmount/set/mount
works and is the sequence covered here.
